{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: archive alarm recovery at exact head a138c30c8ae28faad3dba5db4f358a959fff749c, tree 2f2de129dd9ec582cf7f5138450229a10392326e (review request 891e8254, promise d554067a, implementation 379ed6b2 / dccf9dbe). This approves only the bounded local repair. Parent ea2bfc00 / 610cea67 stays open, and this review makes no hosted claim. The head's parent chain starts at kernel head 6c15e85f, which has now landed as Main d2bd2e8b (tree 3160c521, the same tree as 6c15e85f). So this head's tree is the result of merging it into Main. It may land. Deployment is planner-owned. Please read the residual risk below before the deploy.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-24-archive-alarm-recovery.md (artifact 4d7c71da), first and in full. Then I read the six changed paths from 6c15e85f, the Wakes arbitration in outbox.ts, ArchiveDriver.wake and #pass, the row wake index, the two evidence receipts and the raw gate log.\n\nWhat changed. From 6c15e85f there are two commits. dc1be5821f changes packages/scope/src/object.ts, packages/scope/test/archive-driver.test.ts and two seal values in scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs. a138c30c changes only the note and adds gate.json. The source and test hashes at the head match affected.json (object.ts 28c7ef5c, test 701eedf6).\n\nThe request's conditions, checked in the source:\n- Earlier alarm kept. Recovery reads getAlarm and calls Wakes.set only when the platform alarm is null or later than the archive wake (object.ts:366). An earlier alarm is never touched. This also keeps a platform retry that Wakes does not own.\n- Retry backoff kept. Wakes.set (outbox.ts) sets the earliest stored wake: deadline, dispatch, send, driver, maintenance and archive. That is the same arbitration that the alarm's final step and the constructor already use. Recovery never computes a time of its own, so it cannot shorten a backoff. The witness checks the exact future value, 4070908801000.\n- Active handler. alarm() raises #alarmRunning around the whole #invoke(\"alarm\"), including its final Wakes.set. Nested ingress inside the alarm, such as dispatch, checks the counter before and after the getAlarm await, and returns.\n- Revision fence. Every alarm set goes through schedulingAlarm, which bumps #alarmRevision. A recovery that started before any other set (including the alarm's final one) sees a changed revision after its await, and does nothing.\n- Coalescing. One in-flight promise (#archiveAlarmRecovery) is shared. It is cleared in finally, and a failure is swallowed, so the read's answer is unchanged.\n- Quiet scope. If ArchiveDriver.wake() is null, recovery returns before any async lookup. That covers no registry, a disabled driver, a stopped row (archive-row.ts:235) and an idle row.\n- No archive work on reads. Recovery does not call run(), the host or the journal state. The witness asserts that the row, head and every scripted host counter are unchanged until the real alarm runs, and that the real alarm then confirms the same pending commit.\n\nEvidence:\n- Control (affected.json). With only the nativeBirth recovery call removed, the driver file gave 13 passed and 1 failed. The named assertion was \"a read must re-arm the exact future archive backoff\" (archive-driver.test.ts:96). Production bytes were restored to the same SHA-256. The later-reset assertion, added after the control, passed in the final 14-case run. Of the witness's four alarm checks, the \"earlier alarm\" one would also pass with no recovery. The other three fail without it.\n- Gate. All 29 recorded evidence hashes in gate.json match their raw files under /private/tmp/artroom-archive-wake-gate-evidence-20261024-v1. The raw stages/test.log reads \"Test Files 493 passed | 1 skipped (494)\" and \"Tests 3521 passed | 3 skipped (3524)\". The new witness passed at line 454069. The Node blocks report 39 of 39 and 55 of 55. Every stage exited 0. The gate ran on dc1be5821f, and the head differs from it only in the note and gate.json, so docs/testing.md:597 needs no rerun. The callback reporting gaps and the EPERM pre-body launch are kept as recorded, not relabelled.\n\nDocument checks at a138c30c:\n- git diff --check from 6c15e85f is clean;\n- check-commit-messages from e63b2a7b reports 8 checked, 0 flagged;\n- section citations on an export report 1,877 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved. The script is identical to Main's.\n\nArchitecture: the repair sits at the object boundary. It reuses the single Wakes arbitration, adds no timer or archive state, and runs no archive pass from a read. The cost is one small meta-row read on each ingress for an archive scope. A getAlarm runs only while a wake is stored. A lost alarm on an object with no traffic stays lost until the next ingress or activation, as the note says.\nSecurity: I found no defect. Recovery reads no request data and runs before argument checks and authority work, so a caller cannot steer it. At most it causes one getAlarm per in-flight window. It writes the alarm only when the alarm is missing or later than the stored wake, and only to the arbitrated time. No route, credential, authority, gate class or host I/O changes. Both object.ts seal slots change to the same maintained parser value, and the entry-point Node block passed in the gate.\nSimplification: about 30 lines of logic in object.ts. Reading before writing avoids an alarm write on every ingress, and it keeps a platform retry that Wakes does not own. Nonblocking: the counter and revision fences have no direct test. By my reading, without them the worst case is one redundant re-arm, which the alarm's final arbitration corrects.\n\nFindings: none blocking.\n\nResidual risk for the deploy, from the planner's tail a (0a3c76f7; SHA-256 checked; Eastern times from event timestamps). This is my reading of the e63 code against tail data, not a read of stored state.\n- Alarm events in this tail carry no logs: 7 alarm events, 0 with logs. Both archive diagnostics appear on RPC events at the same object and second: nativeBirth on 7efb5613 at 04:23:16, and source on c7a17b72 at 06:14:12, next to an ok alarm at 06:14:12. So an alarm without a diagnostic here does not show that no pass ran.\n- On 7efb5613 an alarm finished ok at 05:06:11 on version 9348ab6f: 287 ms wall time, 2 ms CPU time. This was after the 04:23:20 cancel. Its final Wakes.set includes the archive wake. From 05:06:12 to 06:18:06 the object served 549 RPC events (543 observed, 5 source, 1 deliver) and no alarm. So at the end of that alarm, the archive wake was probably null (a stopped, disabled or idle row), or later than 06:18, or the set failed. In each case this recovery would not restart Room A: a null wake makes it return, and a later wake gives the same alarm. The 2 ms CPU time matches c7a17b72's alarm, whose diagnostic is a row-load return on a row stopped with reason limit. That match is an inference only.\n- This bears on question 2 of 0a3c76f7. Builder assert ab7131a0, filed after the request, reaches the same limit: this head repairs only a wake that is not stopped and whose platform alarm is missing or later. It cannot recover a row stopped with reason limit, and the tails do not show that it will recover Room A. I agree. The note's diagnosis paragraph predates that evidence. The parent report, not this child, should reconcile it, together with the follow-ups in 6ffc771a.\nNo hosted acceptance or cancellation cause is claimed or approved here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4d7c71da291713763aa23644866565c7775fb554","binding":"assigned","head":"a138c30c8ae28faad3dba5db4f358a959fff749c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0a3c76f733640e713630d5144227d57e04d21bdf\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d554067ad4be45e54f13d6023a472231888e4152\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ab7131a06e52a45210abf8b109046ae2daa32b35\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dccf9dbee4a80cecdf8c4a6f481b496bc75a5397\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:aa85041d938c4c6dbe829a34717579562189d284","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}